HYPERBOLIC HALF-POLYGONS — cut a {p,q} face in two, enumerate what the halves tile
==================================================================================
The hyperbolic half of AL's half-polygon idea, after the spherical one. Same model: a fixed-angle,
fixed-side tile; the vertex rule is EUCLIDEAN (angles close a full turn exactly) because on a
fixed-angle tiling the curvature sits in the FACES; `tileGeometry: "hyperbolic"` in the palette makes
gen_alphabet require the tile's angles to fall SHORT of the flat sum by its area.

What differs from the sphere, and it is the whole engineering difference: a hyperbolic tiling never
closes. There is no area bound and no finite development. What ships is the QUOTIENT plus per-dart data
the client re-develops under the live view — `alpha` (the turn), `elen` (the edge length), `drawn` (all
1, since every edge of a tiling is a real boundary). `hyperbolicDevelopClient` already preferred those
three over its regular-faces derivation, for the Schwarz boards, so nothing client-side was needed.

PALETTES
  hyp-45-half  D=10  angles [1,2,1] = 36-72-36   edges [S,S,D]        achiral
    The {4,5} square (five meet at a vertex, so corners are 72) cut by a diagonal. The diagonal's ends
    are {4,5} VERTICES, so no vertex can become a T-junction and EVERY choice of diagonal tiles.
  hyp-37-half  D=28  angles [2,7,4] = 25.714-90-51.429  edges [alt,half,edge]  CHIRAL
    The {3,7} triangle cut by an altitude. Scalene, so the mirror image is its own alphabet symbol —
    this palette runs only because of the sided-classes repair of 2026-08-13.

SIDES, derived by the polar law of cosines and never typed in
  {4,5}: S = 1.253739326, D = 1.684964163
  {3,7}: half = 0.545274832, alt = 0.903799891, edge = 1.090549664
  ⇒ AL's claim that halving a triangle leaves the long side EXACTLY twice the short one is confirmed:
    2 x 0.545274832 = 1.090549664 to every digit carried. (cosh(2x) = 2cosh(x)^2 - 1 with
    cosh(half) = 1.152389 and cosh(edge) = 1.656027.)
  ⇒ EXTERNAL CROSS-CHECK: 0.545274832 is also the second edge class of Marek's independently produced
    (2,3,7) Schwarz corpus (public/schwarz-hyp: [0.283128153368, 0.545274831754, 0.620671737556]).
    It has to be — the (2,3,7) triangle is a barycentric piece of {3,7}, so one of its sides IS half a
    {3,7} edge — and agreeing to 9 figures with a corpus produced by a different pipeline is the only
    external check available on this side of the curvature split.

RESULTS
  hyp-45-half   k=2: 76    k=3: 40    k=4: 5835        total 5951
  hyp-37-half   k=6:  9    k=7: 54    k=8:   22        total   85
  All 6,036 develop, ZERO failures.

  ⚑ {3,7} IS EMPTY BELOW k=6, AND THAT IS THE BOARD, NOT A SHORT RUN. A first search to k=4 returned
  nothing and looked like a failure. It is a parity fact: each half-triangle's corner at a {3,7} vertex
  is flanked by one FULL edge and one HALF-edge, so the two kinds must alternate round the vertex — and
  7 is odd, so a {3,7} vertex can never be left entirely uncut. The alphabet says the same thing: no
  vertex configuration of seven base corners exists. hypHalfKGaps() reports no gap INSIDE the range, so
  a surface never presents k<6 as missing data.

CERTIFICATION — a proof at the vertex, not a sample
  A bounded patch is developed per record in the hyperboloid model and two things are checked: every
  INTERIOR vertex closes to exactly 2*pi, and every face is congruent to the tile. That is sufficient —
  the developing map is then a local isometry of a simply connected complex with no excess or deficit
  anywhere, so it cannot fold over itself and no overlap test is needed.
    worst side error   {4,5} 2.89e-07 over 5,951 records   {3,7} 1.19e-09 over 85
    min patch          28 closed vertices / 141 faces ({4,5}), 76 / 151 ({3,7})
  ⚑ The first gate was 1e-7 and rejected good tilings. A 600-instance patch reaches cosh r = 170 and the
  frames get there through dozens of matrix products, so the developed lengths drift ~1e-7. The gate is
  now 1e-5 and every record SHIPS its own worst error, so a real shape failure — off by whole units —
  still cannot pass. Measurement reported, gate generous: the right way round.

  NOT AS STRONG AS THE SPHERE. Each spherical board's count was reproduced exactly by an independent
  group computation. There is no such check here: a hyperbolic board has infinitely many tilings, so
  there is no census to reproduce. These are certified LOCALLY (every vertex, every face) and shipped as
  "reproduced" on that basis, which is a weaker claim than the spherical boards carry.

ON THE SHELF
  Hyperbolic -> Tilings -> Board -> "Halved {p,q} faces": {4,5} halved, {3,7} halved.
  A record is a `HypPolyPattern`, so it re-develops through the disk renderer already in place — no new
  component. k<=3 of {4,5} and all of {3,7} are eager (0.6 MB); {4,5} k=4 is 21.4 MB and lazy.
  ⚑ Both half-tile families (spherical too) briefly borrowed the 3.4.n.4 prefix so `subOf`/`familyOfSub`
  would need no changes — and the shelf then filed them under a "3.4.N.4 BOARDS" heading, which is
  false. They now have their own prefixes ("sph-", "hph-") and their own family headings.
  ⚑ `certified` had to be stamped: without it every selection pays a doomed buildDirichletDomain attempt
  on the main thread (median 210 ms, up to 1.2 s). 5,005 of 6,036 certify; the other 1,031 take the 2D path.

CERTIFIED FLAG — final (2026-08-14)
  All 6,036 records stamped, 0 unstamped.
    5,005 certified for the per-pixel Dirichlet renderer
    1,031 on the 2D developed path (1,030 of them in the {4,5} k=4 slice, 17.6% of it)
  The k=4 shard took 2,038 s to stamp. The failure is always the same and is not a defect: the deck
  orbit has to develop to Rdev = 2*RD + 2*rMaxTile + margin, and past tanh(Rdev/2) > 0.99995 the
  Poincare positions collide in the dedup grid, so a certificate there would be a lie. Deeper quotients
  push past that rim. Capability metadata, not catalogue policy: every one of these tilings is real and
  renders either way — the flag only spares the client a doomed attempt (median 210 ms, up to 1.2 s on
  the main thread) before it falls back.

THIRD BOARD: {3,8}, AND THE PARITY PREDICTION TESTED (2026-08-14, later)
  The {3,7} floor at k=6 was explained by parity: each half-triangle's corner at a vertex is flanked by
  one FULL edge and one HALF-edge, the two must alternate round the vertex, and 7 is odd — so no {3,7}
  vertex can be left uncut. That predicts {3,8}, with EVEN valence, should admit an uncut vertex and
  have tilings at lower k. Falsifiable, and it held:
    * the alphabet contains the uncut vertex — eight base corners of 45 degrees, alternating tile and
      mirror-twin (T38'@0/T38@2, both 2 units), summing to 16 = D. {3,7} has no such configuration.
    * the floor drops from k=6 to k=3: 4 tilings at k=3 and 56 at k=4, 60 in all, zero develop failures,
      worst side error 9.65e-08.
  Honest limit of the claim: it did NOT drop to k=1 or k=2, which are still empty. What was predicted and
  confirmed is the uncut vertex and the direction of the floor, not a specific value.
  Sides: half 0.764285460, alt 1.224226224, edge 1.528570919 — and edge = 2 x half again, a third
  independent confirmation of AL's 2:1 observation.

FOURTH BOARD: {5,4}, AND A CERTIFICATE REPLACED (2026-08-14, later)
  The hyperbolic twin of the dodecahedron: four pentagons meet at a {5,4} vertex so its corners are 90,
  a pentagon has no halving diagonal, so the cut is the MIRROR and the halves are 45-90-90-90
  QUADRILATERALS. Chiral. Sides 1.061275061905, half of it, and the mirror segment 1.469351744368 —
  the long edge exactly twice the short one for the FOURTH time. Deficit 45 deg = pi/4.
  61 tilings: k=3: 4, k=4: 38, k=5: 19.

  ⚑ CHIRALITY WAS DECIDED ON THE ANGLE WORD ALONE, AND THAT WAS WRONG. This tile's angles are
  [1,2,2,2], whose reversal is a ROTATION of itself, so mirror_expand called it achiral and added no
  twin — leaving the palette unable to express either handedness. gen_alphabet then REFUSED to generate
  rather than under-report, and that refusal is what found it. The test now runs on the (angle, edge)
  word: an edged tile can have an achiral angle word and still be chiral through its sides. Regression:
  every existing palette regenerates with identical tile / config / entry counts and A6 PASS, and
  make check-regular is byte-identical.

  ⚑ THE PATCH CERTIFICATE WAS REPLACED, because its verdict could not be trusted. It developed a bounded
  patch and measured it, and identifying "the same vertex" reached by two paths needs a dedup tolerance —
  but hyperboloid coordinates grow like e^r, so no tolerance both avoids splitting one vertex in two far
  out and avoids merging two into one. On {5,4} the flood accepted 61 of 61 at a 120-instance patch, 60
  at 200, 38 at 350 and 2 at 600, reporting "vertex overshoots a full turn", which reads like a geometry
  error and was not one. Absolute, relative and Poincare keys each failed in their own way.
    What settled it: multiplying the transforms around each cycle needs no dedup at all, and proved all
    61 close exactly at every vertex AND every face. So that IS the certificate now:
      vertex — the darts round a vertex are related by pure rotation, so the star closes iff the angles
               close a full turn; the quotient is folded, so the test is that a cycle sums to 2*pi/m for
               a whole number m, not to 2*pi
      face   — the product of rot(alpha).medge(rho) round the face cycle, raised to its wrap factor, is
               the identity, and the angles and lengths it meets are the tile's
    Every vertex closing and every face being the tile makes the developing map a local isometry of a
    simply connected complex with no excess or deficit, so it embeds; a patch adds nothing.
    Residuals improved by seven orders of magnitude and the run is instant instead of 105 s:
      {4,5} 2.24e-15   {3,7} 3.07e-16   {3,8} 1.72e-15   {5,4} 1.41e-15   (was 2.89e-07 on {4,5})

  BOARD TOTALS: {4,5} 5951, {3,7} 85, {3,8} 60, {5,4} 61 = 6,157 hyperbolic half-tilings.
